# A box handed to a parameter is still a box inside the body (ruled
# 2026-09-30). `step` and `plus` bind anything at their one position, so the
# box gets in, and the refusal comes where the body hands it to something that
# takes a value: `seen`, which has no arm a box can match, and `+`. Until the
# ruling the checker lost the box at the parameter, and each of these died at
# run time instead, `seen` with "no overload of `seen` matches these
# arguments". `twice` hands its box on to `plus` without reading it, so the
# refusal inside `plus` names the call where the box went in.
fn seen 7
  "seven"

fn seen 8
  "eight"

fn step x
  seen x

fn plus x
  x + 1

fn twice x
  plus x

pub fn run xs
  step xs[1]!

pub play = print (run [7]) .> (_ -> print (twice (effect 5)))
